perturbations.a:
I possess a program in which there are about 10 tasks operating at the same time. They are all invoking the same function with different values, and I want to know how long it takes for each one of my tasks to perform this function. However, it seems to me that I can't use something like that :

A:=Clock;  
MyFunction(...);  
B:=Clock;  
Time:= B-A;  

Indeed, I think this would not return the "real" CPU Time, but the time elapsed between the beginning and the end of the function, which is not correct, because my tasks could be switched when they're performing the function. So, I'm wondering if there is a way to know the "real" CPU time of each one of my tasks, i.e. the time they truly spent using the CPU?  

I think you need the provisions of Annex D.14 :  

"This subclause describes a language-defined package to gauge execution time."  

"The execution time or CPU time of a given task is defined as the time spent by the system executing that task, including the time spent executing run-time or system services on its behalf."  

This is specified in Ada 2005 and Ada 2012. Whether it's available in your compiler on your operating platform is another issue! For example, "Execution_Time is not supported in this configuration" on Mac OS X (GNAT GPL 2013, GCC 4.8).

