perturbations.a:
I possess table training_agendas

* id (int, pk)

* course_identifier (varchar)

* beginning_date (date)

* concluding_date (date)

* year_identifier (int, fk)

and years

* id (int, pk)

* value (int)

Now I wish to construct a search query using the TrainingSchedule eloquent model. I wish to search by course_identifier (using like) and beginning_date(optional) based on month (dropdownlist on form) and year (optional)

optional conditions means I will verify if the form data for the fields are filled on the search form. If not I will merely bypass those conditions.

How can I accomplish this using Eloquent ORM? if not, how to do it in another manner?


You can create a query over multiple calls. So something like the following will likely do what you're seeking:

// initiate query off with a non-optional query
$results = TrainingSchedule::whereLike('course_identifier', Input::get('course_identifier'));

// only if we were sent a beginning date, add it to the query
if (Input::has('beginning_date')) {
    $results->whereStartDate(Input::get('beginning_date'));
}

// if we were sent a month, ensure beginning date's month is the same month as the one passed in
if (Input::has('month')) {
    $results->where(DB::raw('MONTH(`beginning_date`)'), Input::get('month'));
}

// only if we were sent a year, add it to the query
if (Input::has('year')) {
    $results->whereHas('year', function ($query) { $query->whereValue(Input::get('year')); });
}

 // retrieve actual results from db
$results = $results->get();

