perturbations.a:
My objective is to retain the details for an designated string in Parse.com. I opted to devise an encoding for designated text for my visuals that functions by substituting any string {X} in brackets with the matching image. For instance:  

Picture of 2 colorless mana: {X}  

Should generate an designated string where {X} is substituted by an image. This is what I've attempted:  

NSString *formattedText = @"This will cost {2}{PW}{PW} to cast.";  
NSRegularExpression *regex = [NSRegularExpression regularExpressionWithPattern:@"(?<=\\{)[^}]+(?=\\})" options:NSRegularExpressionAnchorsMatchLines  
                                                                         error:nil];  
NSArray *matches = [regex matchesInString:formattedText  
                                  options:kNilOptions  
                                    range:NSMakeRange(0, formattedText.length)];  
NSMutableAttributedString *designatedString = [[NSMutableAttributedString alloc] initWithString:formattedText];  
for (NSTextCheckingResult *result in matches)  
{  
    NSString *match = [formattedText substringWithRange:result.range];  
    NSTextAttachment *imageAttachment = [NSTextAttachment new];  
    imageAttachment.image = [UIImage imageNamed:[NSString stringWithFormat:@"Mana%@.png", match]];  
    NSAttributedString *replacementForTemplate = [NSAttributedString attributedStringWithAttachment:imageAttachment];  
    [designatedString replaceCharactersInRange:result.range  
                          withAttributedString:replacementForTemplate];  
}  
[_textView setAttributedText:designatedString];  

There are two issues with this method currently:  

* The brackets aren't substituted, only the text inside of them.  

* The range for each match is altering because the string itself is altering, and it gets more inaccurate with each substitution whose original text was of length > 1. Here's what it resembles:  

Two issues:  

Brackets aren't substituted. That's because you're utilizing assertions, which aren't counted as part of the match. The match you're making with your pattern only contains the items inside the brackets. Use this pattern instead:  

\{([^}]+)\}  

That's: match a bracket, succeeded by one or more items that aren't closing brackets in a capture group, succeeded by a closing bracket. The entire match includes the brackets now.  

That introduces another issue, though -- you're utilizing the enclosed parts to select the substitute image. Small alteration to fix this: the internal capture group holds that data, now, rather than the entire group. The capture group's length tells you the range of the substring you need.  

NSUInteger lengthOfManaName = [result rangeAtIndex:1].length;  
NSString manaName = [match substringWithRange:(NSRange){1, lengthOfManaName}];  
imageAttachment.image = [UIImage imageNamed:[NSString stringWithFormat:@"Mana%@.png", manaName]];  

Second issue: length of string is altering. Just iterate backwards :  

for (NSTextCheckingResult *result in [matches reverseObjectEnumerator])  
{  
    //...  
}  

Adjustments to ranges towards the end of the string now won't influence earlier ranges.

