The phrase

#define __HI(x) *(1+(int*)&x)

is specified in Sun's math collection , in fdlibm.h . It is utilized in most applications of Sun's math collection, e.g., in its execution of sin(x)

double y[2],z=0.0;
    int n, ix;

    /* High term of x. */
    ix = __HI(x);

    /* |x| ~< pi/4 */
    ix &= 0x7fffffff;
    if(ix <= 0x3fe921fb) return __kernel_sin(x,z,0);

In the code above, variable x is of category double. Can any one clarify to me the syntax the phrase in __HI(x) . The documentation of Sun's collection states __Hi(x) is

"the high segment of a double x (sign,exponent,the first 21 significant
  bits)".

I just do not grasp the syntax of *(1+(int*)&x) , and why it relates to x 's upper segment. Any explanation?


This:

*(1+(int*)&x)

Means: Take the address of x ( &x ), cast it as a reference-to-int (int*) , add 1, and dereference the resulting reference.  Assuming that Sun is utilizing this structure , this means that the value in storage appears as:

If sizeof(int) is 32 bits, this will locate the position of the double in storage, then increment the reference to the next int , which holds the sign, exponent, and first 21 bits of the number.

