    return (from o in db.Orders  
            orderby o.CreatedDate descending  
            select o)  
            .Skip(pageIndex * recordCount)  
            .Take(recordCount)  
            .ToList();  
}  

I need to output the details of the order and the user who generated it:  

foreach (var o in FetchLatestOrders(0, 10))  
{  
    Console.WriteLine("{0} {1}", o.Code, o.Customer.Name);  
}  

This results in a SQL query to retrieve the orders and one query for each order to retrieve the customer. Is it feasible to enhance the query so that it retrieves the orders and its customer in one SQL query?  

Thanks  

UDPATE: By recommendation of sirrocco I modified the query like this and it operates. Only one select query is produced:  

public IList  
FetchLatestOrders(int pageIndex, int recordCount)  
{  
    var options = new DataLoadOptions();  
    options.LoadWith  
    (o => o.Customer);  
    using (var db = new DatabaseDataContext())  
    {  
        db.LoadOptions = options;  
        return (from o in db.Orders  
                orderby o.CreatedDate descending  
                select o)  
                .Skip(pageIndex * recordCount)  
                .Take(recordCount)  
                .ToList();  
    }  
}  

Thanks sirrocco.  

Something else you can perform is EagerLoading. In Linq2SQL you can utilize LoadOptions : More on LoadOptions One VERY strange thing about L2S is that you can establish LoadOptions only before the initial query is sent to the Database.

