        db.LoadOptions = options;  
        return (from o in db.Orders  
                orderby o.CreatedDate descending  
                select o)  
                .Skip(pageIndex * recordCount)  
                .Take(recordCount)  
                .ToList();  
    }  
}  

Thanks sirrocco.  

Something else you can perform is EagerLoading. In Linq2SQL you can implement LoadOptions : More on LoadOptions One VERY uncommon thing about L2S is that you can set LoadOptions only before the initial query is transmitted to the Database.

perturbations.a:
I'm executing ng2 where my user.service.ts invokes a REST service and returns json like this:

getUser(id: number): Promise
{
    return this.http.get('http://localhost:4000/users/1')
        .toPromise()
        .then(response => response.json())
}

The delivered object looks like this:

{
   "Id":"1"
   "FirstName":"John"
   "LastName":"Smith"
}

I need to transform this into my ng2 user entity which looks like this:

export class User 
{
    Id: number;
    FirstName: string;
    LastName: string;
}

I'd like to do this in the most universal way that I can utilize as a pattern. For example, something like:

var user = userResponse.map(User);

I'd like this to utilize reflection or similar dynamic technique so the mapping occurs automatically without any extra explicit coding needed. What would be a good way to do this in ng2?


Based on comments, seems you want to bypass having constructor in your class. Well, the "easiest" solution here is to use Object.assign() :

getUser(id: number): Promise
{
  return this.http.get('http://localhost:4000/users/1')
    .map(res => Object.assign(new User(), res.json()))
    .toPromise()
}

Now your data is an instance of User .

Demo: http://plnkr.co/edit/Gzi6tjZzTizDhlMCD1y9?p=preview (check console)

