Runnable runnable = () -> System.out.println("Thread name: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());

Executor executor;

executor = Runnable::run;  
executor.execute(runnable);  

So, I can't comprehend how method reference in this case enables it to create an Executor and how is it feasible to invoke execute(Runnable command) if it was not executed. In general how does the method reference functions in this case behind the scenes?  

An Executor satisfies the definition of a functional interface, in that it has one abstract method. Namely this one:  

void execute(Runnable command)  

So what we require in order to implement this functional interface is a method which works on a Runnable and returns nothing. command -> command.run(), or Runnable::run for short, is an illustration of a method that will do this.  

The ensuing three bits of code are equivalent:  

executor = Runnable::run;  

executor = (Runnable command) -> command.run();  

executor = new Executor() {  
    public void execute(Runnable command) {  
        command.run();  
    }  
}

