I am attempting to filter a list by a search string. It states in the doc on the blue note that:  

* IQueryable provides you the database provider implementation of Contains.  

* IEnumerable provides you the .NET Framework implementation of Contains.  

* The default configuration of SQL Server instances is case-insensitive.  

* Using ToUpper to create an explicit case-insensitive call should be evaded because it has a performance penalty.  

My filtering is as follows:  

IQueryable  
customers =   
    from u in _context.Users  
    where (u.Customer != null && u.IsActive)  
    select u;  

if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(searchString))  
{  
    customers = customers.Where(s => s.Email.Contains(searchString));  
}  

This solution however is case-sensitive, and I don't precisely comprehend why: since I'm utilizing IQueryable, it should utilize the database provider implementation, that is case-insensitive by default, right?  

I'm utilizing EF Core 2 and presently just executing a local MSSQLLocalDB.  

starting from version 2.1 of the EF Core, you can utilize HasConversion(). But the data in the database will be preserved in lowercase:  

builder.Property(it => it.Email).HasConversion(v => v.ToLowerInvariant(), v => v);  

I resolved a similar issue. This alteration resolved all my dilemmas.

