perturbations.a:
This is nearly directly from some of the instance on the Next.js site.

function HomePage(props) {  
  return (  
Welcome to {props.data.name} Next.js!  
);  
}  

// This gets invoked on every request  
export async function getServerSideProps(context) {  
  const res = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/test');  
  const data = await res.json();  

  // Convey data to the page via props  
  return { props: { data } };  
}  

export default HomePage;  

That code operates great locally of course. But how can I change the http://localhost:3000 with something "dynamic" depending on where the site is functioning? The site could be functioning locally (localhost), a preview URL created by Vercel (something-random.now.sh), another Vercel URL (something-else.now.sh), or a custom URL (mydomain.com). Is there a method to ascertain how to make that call the Next.js /api on all of those without a .env file? With the .env file I wouldn't know how to establish it for the "dynamic" URLs created by Vercel for previews (and other Vercel URLs).  

Thanks!  

You can obtain the URL of the deployment by setting the System Environment Variable VERCEL_URL filled by Vercel.  

1. Visit your project configuration page in Vercel.  

2. In the "Environment Variables" section, enter VERCEL_URL as Name, leave the Value empty, and click Add.  

3. Repeat for the Preview environment.  

You can discover more about System Environment Variables in  
  this Vercel  
  Docs.  

Then, within getServerSideProps, you can obtain the URL with process.env.VERCEL_URL.  

Please note that with this method, you can obtain the preview URL(something-random.now.sh) and production URL(something-else.now.sh) under different environments. But when you visit your custom domain(exmaple.com), you will still receive the production URL.  

This resolution may be acceptable if you are merely utilizing your custom URL but not Vercel's production URL:  

let baseUrl = 'http://localhost:3000'  
if(process.env.Vercel_URL) {  
  baseUrl = process.env.Vercel_URL === 'https://something-else.now.sh'? 'https://exmaple.com': process.env.Vercel_URL  
}  

If you require both custom URL and Vercel's production URL to function, you might need another resolution.

