I was looking for a method to incorporate an ellipsis in a C# path, and discovered an solution here on stackoverflow: C# Path Ellipsis without Win32 API call

Using the RTM variants of VS2010 and .Net 4.0, I was unsuccessful in making the recommended technique function. I browsed the 'Net and located sample code that employs the identical technique, but it failed in the same manner.

You can observe the string I'm attempting to abbreviate in my code below.

After invoking the MeasureText method, both the input string (OriginalName) and the output string (ellipsisedName) appear like this:

d:\abcd\efgh\ijkl\mnop\qrst\...\test.txt\0F\GHIJ\KLMN\OPQR\STIV\WXYZ\test.txt

Two issues:

1) The resulting string is messed up (the path is shortened as anticipated, but is trailed by what looks like a C-style terminating null and a portion of the original path).

2) My original string is altered to be identical to the output string.

Am I making an error?

namespace WindowsFormsApplication2 {
   public partial class Form1 : Form {
      public Form1()
      {
         InitializeComponent();

         string OriginalPath = @"d:\abcd\efgh\ijkl\mnop\qrst\uvwx\yzAB\CDEF\GHIJ\KLMN\OPQR\STIV\WXYZ\test.txt";
         string ellipsisedPath = OriginalPath;

         Size proposedSize = new Size(label1.Width, label1.Height);

         TextRenderer.MeasureText(ellipsisedPath, label1.Font, proposedSize, TextFormatFlags.ModifyString | TextFormatFlags.PathEllipsis);
      }
   }
}


Holy moly, you've discovered a whopper of a bug. The P/Invoke utilized inside the TextRenderer class that invokes DrawTextEx() is flawed. That API function is writing back into the string, which it is permitted to do since the cchText argument is a LPTSTR, not a LPCTSTR. That ruins the .NET string content for both variables because the string is interned.

The bug isn't exclusive to .NET 4.0, I observe it incorrectly in the ReferenceSource for .NET 3.5 SP1 as well and can reproduce it on VS2008. The issue lies in the internal WindowsGraphics.MeasureText function. You can report the bug at connect.microsoft.com.

A potential workaround is to modify the string so it gets replicated and can't impact the original:

string ellipsisedPath = OriginalPath + '\0';

But the superior workaround in this scenario is to simply not pass the ModifyString option, it serves no function. Which is safer too, there is still a chance of damaging the garbage collected heap with the first workaround. The fix for Microsoft is similarly straightforward, it should merely mask out the ModifyString option. It is documented to have no effect.

