I am entering Spring now and I have a query. Let's assume we have an interface IControl and we have three realizations of it - e.g. Button, Label, Checkbox.  
The user will input a number N at runtime and I should create N controls of a particular type (Button, Label or Checkbox). The issue is I want this particular type to be configured before the program is executed. For instance, I configure in my spring.xml that I want buttons, then the program is executed, the user inputs 10 and I create 10 buttons.

How should I proceed configuring this?

P.S This is just a learning example I invented.

Best regards,  
Petar  

First of all, allow me to mention that Spring is not designed to do this. Spring aims to provide an unobtrusive method of wiring your components together. It's not meant to be employed for generating objects at runtime.  

The correct method  

The correct method would be to devise a factory class. You can establish the defaults of this using Spring at startup, then request the factory for instances at runtime.  

The intrusive method  

If you truly want to employ Spring, you can do intrusively by asking the applicationContext for an instance of your control.  

By default, all Spring beans are singletons, but you can transform them into prototypes.  

Disclaimer: code not executed, but should be something like this:  

Applicationcontext.xml  

Code    

public class controlCreator implements ApplicationContextAware {  

  private ApplicationContext appContext;  

  public Control createButton() {  
    // since this bean is prototype, a new one will be created like this  
    return getApplicationContext().getBean("myButton");  
  }  

  // ... getter and setter for applicationContext  

}  

The unobtrusive method  

If you truly want to employ Spring, and you genuinely want to use it unobtrusively, you will have to utilize Method Injection. Inject a factory method into your class.  

The code would be the following:  

Applicationcontext.xml  

Code  

public class controlCreator implements ApplicationContextAware {  

  private ApplicationContext appContext;  

  public abstract Control createButton();  

  // ... getter and setter for applicationContext  

}  

This will return the bean when the lookup method is invoked. Since the bean is prototype, it will create a new one.

