i encountered a slight issue in Oracle. I attempt to create a sequence for generating IDs in a table that already contains data in it. I attempt to use the following anonymous block.

declare y varchar2(2000);  
BEGIN  
  SELECT 'CREATE SEQUENCE ID_SEQ MINVALUE 1 MAXVALUE 9999999999 START WITH ' || (max(ID)+1) || ' INCREMENT BY 1 CACHE 20;' INTO y FROM TEST_TABLE;  
  --dbms_output.put_line(y);  
  execute immediate y;  
end;

I receive the following error:

Error report:  
ORA-00911: invalid character  
ORA-06512: at line 5  
00911. 00000 -  "invalid character"

If I run the value of the y variable it functions perfectly. I'm utilizing SQL Developer as input interface and working on a 11g r2 Oracle server. I discovered similar codes where 'INCREMENT BY' parameters were script generated. Can someone clarify my error?

When you execute immediate, the command you perform should not have a semi-colon on the end; that is a command separator in SQL Developer (and SQL*Plus, and other clients), not part of the SQL statement itself.

SELECT 'CREATE SEQUENCE ID_SEQ MINVALUE 1 MAXVALUE 9999999999 START WITH '  
    || (max(ID)+1) || ' INCREMENT BY 1 CACHE 20' INTO y FROM TEST_TABLE;  

This is shown in the examples for plain SQL. Just to aid confuse you though, if you are utilizing PL/SQL within the dynamic SQL, you do still require semi-colons appropriate to PL/SQL itself - though not the executing / you'd use to run it directly from a client. This is shown in other examples.

