perturbations.a:
When I build a new class file in C#, the customary structure is as follows:

using System;  
using System.Collections.Generic;  
using System.Linq;  
using System.Text;  

namespace UnitTest  
{  
    class Class1  
    {  
    }  
}  

StyleCop doesn't appreciate having using directives outside of the namespace, so ideally, I would reorganize my code as such:

namespace UnitTest  
{  
    using System;  
    using System.Collections.Generic;  
    using System.Linq;  
    using System.Text;  

    class Class1  
    {  
    }  
}  

From my days as a Java programmer, I was instructed that it was better to import only the references that were required, rather than the entire package like so:

import foo.bar.MyObject;  

instead of  

import foo.bar.*;  

I know that this can also be accomplished in C#, with the added feature that you can utilize aliases for types (sometimes helpful when working through native code):

using StringBuilder = System.Text.StringBuilder;  

or when utilizing aliases:

using HANDLE = System.IntPtr;  
using HDC = System.IntPtr;  

So my inquiries, regarding best practice and efficiency of using directives:  

1. Is it more efficient to maintain using statements inside a namespace, or is this purely a stylistic choice?  

2. Is it more efficient to only incorporate the necessary items, as opposed to the entire namespace?  


1. There are some fringe cases where it makes a difference but for the majority of cases it is just a stylistic choice.  

2. The using statements merely inform the compiler where it can find the types. It has no effect on the runtime.

